WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:32.780
The rat is a social animal. ...burrows from which highway systems are constructed and along which they meet their associates.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:51.380
They seek food in company and quench their thirst in company and reproduce in aggregate.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:02.280
Our familiarity with the rat is usually based upon its living in close association with humans.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:11.900
We shall, however, be interested in the rat's own home life, in the behavioral maturation of a young rat until it becomes an adult integrated into the rat society.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:23.760
This film was compiled over a six-week period. It was taken at Towson, Maryland, on a quarter-acre tract of land surrounded by a ratproof fence.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.880
Within this pen, the several colonies shown in this film were observed for 27 months.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:41.300
Individual rats were marked with numbered aluminum tags inserted in their ears. They were also marked for sight identification by removing patches of fur.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:03:05.420
Rats construct burrows which form their major locus of orientation. Excavated dirt forms arc-like mounds.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:17.400
Trails connect neighboring burrows.
As the underground tunnel system enlarges, the surface becomes one, fused mound.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.980
Only one-third of the mound of this burrow is shown here.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:31.660
Entrances about which there is no excavated dirt occur toward the periphery of the burrows.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.680
Note the rat entering such a bolthole.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:44.840
Radial extension of tunnels which emerge to the surface, form these boltholes.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.320
Departure from burrow systems is frequently with considerable caution.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:08.420
Trails from burrows may lead through holes and fences, from which the rat can proceed to its source of food,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:15.540
or it may join other rats before it again returns to its home burrow by way of the trailway system.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:27.900
Rats structure their environment in many ways, including the denuding of vegetation.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:35.440
One of the important ways in which rats structure their environment is by plugging entrances into burrows.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:43.200
Here a lactating female is sealing the entrance with dirt and rocks from the inside.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:50.540
The sealing of entrances is most frequently exhibited by lactating females.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:57.620
Low social rank also contributes to the increase and frequency of sealing burrows.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:06.760
After completing her job, the female comes out and inspects her handiwork.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:15.080
Other rats which might have entered and approached her young, pass by.

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:25.080
A single entrance may be used by several rats as the four rats shown here indicate.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:33.980
A female begins to modify the situation by plugging this entrance with grass.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.040
Her work is soon taken over by a male.

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:47.760
Note how this rat pulls on the grass with both teeth and forefeet, and that the forefeet are used alternately as in the overhand pulling of a rope.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.860
Several rats immediately inspect the recently- plugged entrance.

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:20.540
Sticks are also dragged over burrow entrances where they form a partially-effective barrier.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:29.960
Rats are continually altering the appearance of their environment by gnawing on wooden objects.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:41.380
They are agile climbers. Frequently trees at night become festooned with rats.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:55.200
The tail assists in balancing.

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:01.540
Manipulation of food forms a major category of manual abilities.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:12.380
Food objects held in the forepaws are rotated as eating progresses.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:18.360
When the objects cannot be reached with the teeth, they are raked toward the rat with the forefeet.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:35.240
Self-manipulation as in this cleaning of the tail frequently occurs. Included in self-manipulation is the grooming of the fur.

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:53.800
The structuring of the environment by rats produces situations in which social interaction is increased.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:59.940
Trails are one such situation. Trails connect goals.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.680
They lead from the burrow to the source of food.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:20.380
They connect adjoining burrows. Increased contacts also occur where trails from burrows converge and pass through a barrier, as at this fence.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:34.040
The structuring of the environment makes it easier for young to orient between goals.
Note how this mother is followed by one of her young.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:47.160
Trails enable month-old young to find water at some distance from their homes and there to meet strange adults.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:14.480
On the long course of maturation and integration into society, the rat's first associations are with its mother.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.060
She delivers them into the world.

00:09:56.020 --> 00:10:02.660
Naked, blind, and helpless, they would not long survive without her maternal care.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:14.540
By two weeks of age, they are well-furred but their eyes have just begun to open. They rarely venture out of the nest.

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:23.740
It is also during this period of life of the young, that the mother is particularly aggressive

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:29.880
in driving away any other rats which approach that portion of the burrow where her litter is located.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:36.260
She events repels the dominant male of the entire colony of 150 adults as he approaches from the right.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:54.600
Or in this case, she nips a male who attempts to invade her burrow.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:00.760
Between two and three weeks of age, initial explorations within the burrow are begun.

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:06.920
However, if the young venture forth, particularly if they get outside the burrow, the mother will retrieve them.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:24.860
Up to 21 days of age, their only contact with adults is usually with their mother.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:33.980
By the time they are 23 to 25 days of age, they are quite active, make independent excursions within the burrow and in its immediate vicinity.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:40.020
But even here, they frequently remain in close association with their mother.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:48.040
The four-to-five weeks old rat is characterized by hesitancy in leaving its burrow and by dashing motions to and from burrows.

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:55.940
These scenes illustrate the utilization of pre-existing trails by juveniles.

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:04.300
This hypersensitivity of the four to five weeks old rat is also exhibited at a source of food it encountered for the first time.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:24.140
It finally obtains a piece of food and runs away with it. Here again, is the dashing movement by a juvenile in food procurement.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:34.700
Note the five weeks old rat drinking under the drain spout. He dashes away upon the approach of an adult.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:45.260
The beginnings of social adjustment are visible in these scenes of a five weeks old rat upon its first approach to the source of water.

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:56.260
Each time an adult approaches, it darts away. This is unlike the behavior of adults, who much less frequently avoid the approach of another adult.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:06.060
Note how this juvenile rat reacts to adults whom he is first encountering at the source of food.

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:11.140
He darts about the periphery of the group and runs away each time an adult approaches.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:17.400
The adults show much less avoidance of each other despite a rapidly dwindling food supply.

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:27.340
By the time the rat is nearly a year old, it may actively compete over some restricted goal,
as these two males are doing over scent left by an estrus female.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:37.300
Most encounters between adults are relatively placid. The most common behavior is nose-touching.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:49.000
Two individuals approach cautiously and nearly or actually touch noses.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:55.720
Following recognition, each individual goes its own way or the one of lower social rank merely retreats.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:03.660
On guard or boxing behavior may be exhibited by one or both rats in an encounter.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:17.100
A rat which is stationary will frequently run away from a rat in motion.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:28.680
Hip-throwing is a form of threat exhibited by dominant rats.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:42.140
Strife with their fellows accentuates the caution frequently exhibited in departing from the burrow,

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:47.220
and upon returning, they dart back in.

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:54.240
Caution is also exhibited along trails.

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:10.900
Caution is the prime characteristic of rats, as in approaching the food source beyond these steps,

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:19.560
or in coming through a passage which necessitates their passing near the burrow of more dominant rats,

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:29.100
or in approaching a strange object, as these traps.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:40.560
Strange noises, as the rattling of paper by the rat entering the garbage can, causes withdrawal as by the rats on the left.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:16:02.740
This particular burrow system and its radial extensions, housed 13 females, most of whom had litters.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:12.580
They formed the harem of the most dominant male of the population. This dominant male may be recognized by his crumpled, hairless, left ear.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:28.780
At the food source, or by a burrow hole, he roams above his harem's burrow at will. He guards the passage through the fence near his burrow.

00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:36.360
Rats living on the opposite side of the fence must pass through it to obtain food.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:48.160
After one sneaked past him, there is elicited this dirt-pushing, which has erotic implications

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:54.240
and may be exhibited by dominant males following aggressive acts.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:11.900
Finally, he blocks the passage with his body. As males approach this passage, he assiduously chases them away.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:44.680
Only after the dominant male departs can these rats, whose home is farther from the food supply, go back and forth undisturbed.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:02.840
Males who have contended unsuccessfully, possess much scar tissue and often large, open wounds.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:16.060
Note the two males to the left. Such males are frequently forced to live in exposed sub-optimal harborages as under this ladder.

00:18:19.460 --> 00:18:26.400
Although the dominant male was aggressive about his home burrow, he rarely exhibited aggression at the source of food.

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:30.280
Here, he is the central rat in the garbage can.

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:53.200
The securing of food and water consumes a large part of each rat's nightly activity.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:08.440
Water may be lapped up directly or it may be secured by dipping in the forepaw and licking off the water. Or dew may be licked off of grass.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:16.840
When the water supply is ample, several rats may drink together with little antagonism.

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:27.660
When the food supply is ample, little antagonism is exhibited at the source, even though rats from different, local colonies associate there.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:37.600
At a source of food which is ample but restricted in availability, there is usually pushing and mild threats.

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:49.340
Little antagonism exists among rats at an ample and readily available food supply.

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:59.500
Food is continually being removed and transported. Rats of 35 days of age and older transport food.

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:07.860
A single individual may make many consecutive trips. Most of the food is taken to the rat's home burrow.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:23.200
As the food is depleted, more rats aggregate at the food source. Even so, fighting such as it occurs in territorial defense, rarely occurs.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:30.360
The characteristic behavior is an increased irritability in the form of more frequent threats.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:48.600
Fighting may occur as over this last piece of palatable food, a crust of rye bread.

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:58.900
Prior deprivation of water for 48 hours fosters large aggregates.

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:04.400
There is a marked increase in irritability and the frequency of threats.

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:11.140
Social rank influences the ability of maintaining position at the water.

00:21:19.880 --> 00:21:26.700
Males are attracted to the scent of females in estrus. Males inspect and follow females.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:46.280
The female leaves scent at the burrow entrance, over sticks and on the ground. Note the feces.

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:52.080
The scent of the estrus female forms a signpost.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:22:02.700
The male drags his genitalia over the sticks, gnaws upon them, and saturates his fur by rubbing under them.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:17.280
Males inspect the scent about the burrow entrance. A male drags his genitalia over a scent post.

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:31.620
Rocks become scent posts.
Earth saturated with estrus scent is eaten.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:43.620
Other females compete over the priority to inspect the scent. Though they are members of his harem, this male approaches with caution,

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:48.220
as one of the females also rubs against the passage through the fence.

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:10.240
The predominant pre-copulatory behavior elicited by the estrus scent is erotic rolling about burrow entrances.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:24.360
The estrus female may remain just inside the burrow entrance.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:35.060
The male rarely enters after her and then only momentarily.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:53.560
Sexual rolling apparently may elicit an orgasm.

00:23:58.380 --> 00:24:06.060
At least the penis is cleaned, as after copulation.
The receptive female spends considerable time above ground.

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:13.460
The male cleans its penis following mounting. Note the raising of the pelvic region by the female.

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:27.640
The male sometimes falls over. The female, after being frequently mounted, becomes antagonistic toward males.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:36.200
The stress experienced by the female is aggravated by many males joining the pursuit.

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:53.620
As soon as one male descends, another mounts. She finally escapes. When she emerges, the pursuit again ensues.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:25:01.540
Such intensive pursuit only exists at colonies lacking a clearly dominant male.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:14.240
Tension also develops between males. Such chases last many hours.

00:25:14.700 --> 00:25:21.360
This is in contrast to those colonies where a highly dominant male protects his harem from such stress.

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:28.700
As the night wears on, the antagonism of the female toward males increases, as also does the tension among males.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:37.660
When females do manage to reach the water, males prevent them from satisfying their needs.

00:25:43.240 --> 00:25:49.240
In such colonies, having an unstable social structure, the females have a lowered rate of conception,

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:56.000
despite much copulation and even those which conceive, rarely rear their young.

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:21.600
In the past scenes we have shown some of the behaviors exhibited by rats, as well as a few of the situations in which these behaviors take place.

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:28.900
Even so, it is apparent that the life of this so-called lower mammal is quite complex.

